3 Getting Started
*****************

There are two ways to open a review:

‘M-x code-review-start’
     Prompt for the URL of a pull request or merge request and open it.

‘M-x code-review-forge-pr-at-point’
     Open the pull request at point in a Forge or Magit buffer, or the
     one shown in a Forge topic buffer.  *Note Key Binding
     Suggestions::, for a binding.

   The basic workflow:

  1. Move to a line of the diff and press ‘RET’ to write a comment on
     it.  *Note Comments and Replies::.
  2. Press ‘RET’ on a comment that is already on the forge to reply to
     it, or on one of your local comments to edit it.
  3. Press ‘r s f’ to write your overall feedback.
  4. Press ‘r a’ to approve, ‘r r’ to request changes (Github only) or
     ‘r c’ to send your comments without a verdict.  *Note Submitting a
     Review::.

   Your comments stay local until you submit the review, so you can
write them in any order and change them first.


File: code-review.info,  Node: The Review Buffer,  Next: Comments and Replies,  Prev: Getting Started,  Up: Top

4 The Review Buffer
*******************

Each pull request gets its own review buffer, named after the repository
and the number: ‘*Code Review: visor!4*’ for a Gitlab merge request,
‘*Code Review: emacs#12*’ for a Github pull request.  You can have
several reviews open at once, for instance to handle an urgent one in
the middle of another; the commands in a review buffer, and in a comment
buffer started from it, act on that buffer's pull request.

   The review buffer is a Magit section buffer: ‘TAB’ folds and unfolds
a section, ‘n’ and ‘p’ move between sections and ‘M-n’ and ‘M-p’ between
sibling sections, as in Magit.  It has these sections, from top to
bottom:

   • the header: author, title, state, branches, milestone, labels,
     projects, draft state, assignees, suggested reviewers and
     reviewers;
   • the commits, with their CI state on Github;
   • the description;
   • ‘Your Review Feedback’, the overall message of your review;
   • the conversation: comments that are not on a line of the diff;
   • the files changed, each with its diff and the comments on its
     lines.

   Many header lines are commands: press ‘RET’ on the title, the labels,
the milestone or the assignee to change them (*note Editing the Pull
Request::), on an author to visit their profile, and on a suggested
reviewer to request their review.

‘r’
     Show the Transient menu with all review commands
     (‘code-review-transient-api’).

‘RET’
     On a diff line, write a comment on it; on a comment, reply to it;
     on one of your local comments, edit it
     (‘code-review-comment-add-or-edit’).

‘C-c C-s’
     Write a code suggestion for the diff line at point
     (‘code-review-comment-code-suggestion’).  Github only.

‘C-c RET’
     Write a comment on the diff line at point and send it right away,
     outside a review
     (‘code-review-submit-single-diff-comment-at-point’).

‘C-c C-k’
     Delete the local comment or the feedback at point.

‘C-c C-r’
     Add a reaction to the comment or description at point (*note
     Reactions::).

‘C-c C-n’
     Promote the comment at point to a new issue.

‘C-RET’
‘r v’
     Visit the file at point in your local clone (*note Visiting
     Files::).

‘G’
     Reload the pull request from the forge (‘code-review-reload’).

   The Transient menu's ‘Review’, ‘Merge’, ‘Fast track’ and ‘Setters’
groups are described in *note Submitting a Review::, *note Merging::,
*note Saving and Resuming Reviews:: and *note Editing the Pull
Request::.  Its ‘Buffer’ group has ‘r v’ to visit the file at point, ‘r
G’ to reload the pull request and ‘r q’ to close the menu.

   The commands ‘code-review-toggle-display-all-comments’,
‘code-review-toggle-display-top-level-comments’ and
‘code-review-toggle-display-diff-comments’ hide or show comments, which
helps when reading the diff of a busy pull request.

   ‘code-review-comment-jump-next’ and
‘code-review-comment-jump-previous’ move to the next and previous
comment; they have no default binding (*note Key Binding Suggestions::).

   Press ‘RET’ on a commit to review that commit alone, in a separate
‘*Code Review Commit*’ buffer (‘code-review-commit-at-point’).  Press
‘b’ there to go back to the whole pull request.


File: code-review.info,  Node: Comments and Replies,  Next: Submitting a Review,  Prev: The Review Buffer,  Up: Top

5 Comments and Replies
**********************

Writing a comment, a reply or the feedback opens a ‘*Code Review
Comment*’ buffer in Markdown mode.  There is one such buffer, also with
several reviews open, so finish or cancel a comment before starting one
in another review.  It has these keys:

‘C-c C-c’
     Save the comment locally (‘code-review-comment-commit’).  Comments
     written with ‘C-c RET’ and ‘r s c’ are sent right away instead.

‘C-c C-k’
     Cancel (‘code-review-comment-quit’).

‘C-c @’
     Insert a mention of a user, chosen with completion
     (‘code-review-input-mention-user-at-point’).

   Saved comments are shown in the review buffer as local comments.
They are sent with the review (*note Submitting a Review::).  Press
‘RET’ on a local comment to edit it, and ‘C-c C-k’ to delete it.

   ‘C-c C-s’ on a diff line opens the comment buffer with that line in a
‘suggestion’ block, which you change into the code you suggest.  Github
shows it with a button to apply the suggestion.  This is not supported
on Gitlab yet.


File: code-review.info,  Node: Submitting a Review,  Next: Editing the Pull Request,  Prev: Comments and Replies,  Up: Top

6 Submitting a Review
*********************

Press ‘r’ in the review buffer for the Transient menu.  Its ‘Review’
group sends your local comments and replies, the feedback and a verdict:

‘r a’
     Approve (‘code-review-submit-approve’).

‘r r’
     Request changes (‘code-review-submit-request-changes’).  Github
     only.

‘r c’
     Send the comments without a verdict
     (‘code-review-submit-comments’).

   The feedback is the overall message of the review.  Set it with ‘r s
f’ (‘code-review-set-feedback’) or ‘RET’ on ‘Your Review Feedback’, and
delete it with ‘C-c C-k’ there (‘code-review-delete-feedback’).  Github
requires a feedback message to request changes.

   The ‘Fast track’ group sends things without a review:

‘r l’
     Approve with the message in ‘code-review-lgtm-message’
     (‘code-review-submit-lgtm’).

‘r p’
     Send only your replies to existing comments
     (‘code-review-submit-only-replies’).

‘r s c’
     Write a comment on the conversation and send it right away
     (‘code-review-submit-single-top-level-comment’).

‘r s C’
     Write a comment on the diff line at point and send it right away
     (‘code-review-submit-single-diff-comment-at-point’).

   Set ‘code-review-always-restrict-approval?’ to non-‘nil’ to refuse
approving a pull request whose CI state is not successful (Github).


