11 Visiting Files
*****************

‘C-RET’ or ‘r v’ (‘code-review-visit-file-at-point’) on a diff line or a
file heading opens the file in your local clone of the repository, the
worktree Forge knows for it, at the line at point.  When the clone is
not at the pull request's head commit, it offers to check out the pull
request's branch there, but only if the clone has no uncommitted
changes.  Then the code you read, and tools like Eglot, see the pull
request's version.

   Where the file is visited is up to ‘code-review-worktree-function’.
It is called with the pull request and returns the directory to visit
the file in.  The default, ‘code-review-checkout-in-clone’, is the
behavior described above.  Checking out in your clone gets in the way of
your own work, so you may prefer a function that checks the pull request
out in a ‘git worktree’ of its own and sets up what you need there: a
virtual environment, a devcontainer, a language server.  Such a function
can use ‘code-review-local-clone’, which returns the clone Forge knows,
and ‘code-review-pr-head’, which returns the pull request's head commit.
The pull request object also has the ‘owner’, ‘repo’ and ‘number’ slots;
Gitlab merge requests are at ‘refs/merge-requests/N/head’ on the forge,
Github pull requests at ‘refs/pull/N/head’.  For example:

     (defun my-review-worktree (pr)
       "Check PR out in a worktree of its own, next to the clone."
       (let* ((clone (code-review-local-clone pr))
              (tree (expand-file-name
                     (format "../%s-review-%s" (oref pr repo) (oref pr number))
                     clone)))
         (unless (file-directory-p tree)
           (let ((default-directory clone))
             (call-process "git" nil nil nil "fetch" "origin"
                           (format "refs/merge-requests/%s/head"
                                   (oref pr number)))
             (call-process "git" nil nil nil "worktree" "add" "--detach"
                           tree (code-review-pr-head pr))))
         tree))

     (setq code-review-worktree-function #'my-review-worktree)

   ‘RET’ on a binary file, such as an image, downloads it to
‘code-review-download-dir’ and opens that directory in Dired.  ‘C-c C-v’
opens the file on the forge instead.


File: code-review.info,  Node: Forge Support,  Next: Gitlab,  Prev: Visiting Files,  Up: Top

12 Forge Support
****************

Features supported by each forge:

Feature                                Github         Gitlab
---------------------------------------------------------------
Add comments                           yes            yes
Reply to comments                      yes            yes
Send only replies                      yes            yes
Send a review with comments            yes            yes
Approve                                yes            yes
Request changes                        yes            no
Send a single comment                  yes            yes
Send a single diff comment             yes            yes
Code suggestions                       yes            no
Set labels                             yes            yes
Set the assignee                       yes            no
Set the milestone                      yes            no
Edit the title                         yes            yes
Edit the description                   yes            no
Request reviewers                      yes            no
Merge                                  experimental   no
Reactions                              yes            no
Promote a comment to an issue          yes            no
Save and resume an unfinished review   yes            yes
Get and visit binary files             yes            yes
CI state of commits                    yes            no

   Commands for features a forge doesn't support report ‘Not supported
in Gitlab yet.’ rather than failing.


