13.1 Authentication
===================

If you use Forge, reuse its token and skip the rest of this section:

     (setq code-review-auth-login-marker 'forge)

   Otherwise, create a personal access token on Gitlab with the ‘api’
scope, and add a line with your login and the token to your auth source
file, usually ‘~/.authinfo.gpg’:

     machine gitlab.com/api login yourlogin^code-review password TOKEN

   The token is also found when the line names the REST endpoint
instead, ‘gitlab.com/api/v4’, which is where Forge stores its tokens.


File: code-review.info,  Node: Gitlab Instances,  Next: Approving on Gitlab,  Prev: Gitlab Authentication,  Up: Gitlab

13.2 Gitlab Instances
=====================

Each review uses the Gitlab instance of its merge request, so reviews on
gitlab.com and on self-hosted instances work side by side without any
configuration:

   • From Forge (‘code-review-forge-pr-at-point’), the API host is the
     one Forge uses for the repository.
   • From a URL (‘code-review-start’), any
     ‘https://HOST/.../-/merge_requests/N’ URL is recognized.  The API
     host is the one in HOST's entry in ‘forge-alist’, or else
     ‘HOST/api’.

   Ghub finds your username in the Git variable ‘gitlab.HOST.user’, or
else ‘gitlab.user’, as for Forge:

     git config --global gitlab.git.example.com.user yourlogin

   The options ‘code-review-gitlab-host’ and
‘code-review-gitlab-graphql-host’ are only used for reviews saved before
the host was recorded, and for an instance whose API host is not
‘HOST/api’ and not in ‘forge-alist’.  In that case set them, with
‘code-review-gitlab-base-url’, for that instance:

     (setq code-review-gitlab-base-url "git.example.com"       ; MR URLs
           code-review-gitlab-host "git.example.com/api"       ; REST
           code-review-gitlab-graphql-host "git.example.com/api"); GraphQL

   For an instance served over HTTP rather than HTTPS, add it to
‘ghub-insecure-hosts’:

     (setq ghub-insecure-hosts '("git.example.com"))


File: code-review.info,  Node: Approving on Gitlab,  Prev: Gitlab Instances,  Up: Gitlab

13.3 Approving on Gitlab
========================

‘r a’ approves the merge request.  Gitlab's approve API takes no
message, so the feedback set with ‘r s f’ is posted as a separate
comment next to the approval.

   A Gitlab project can require users to enter their password to approve
a merge request; then you are asked for your username and password.

   Requesting changes (‘r r’) is not supported on Gitlab.


